{
  "what_this_is": "How panes size and snap, as DATA. A person can drag a pane's edge down to drag_minimum_px along its split (so it slides nearly out of the way at every screen size); its screen is still laid out at content_minimum_width_px by content_minimum_height_px and the pane shows the part that fits, its cut edges fading out over cut_fade_px so a squashed pane reads as tucked away rather than broken. The sidebar's fit (parts/sidebar/read-data/docked-list-fit.ts) keeps the work at least content_minimum_width_px wide. A new pane splitting another takes new_pane_share_percent of it; a pane added beside or below another opens at added_pane_width_px or added_pane_height_px while every pane keeps the size it has (the arrangement scrolls), and a dragged edge snaps to snap_points_percent of its split when within snap_within_px, settling over snap_ease_ms; a pane's own height in a row (or width in a stack) snaps the same way to points of the split's height. The arrow keys move a pane edge to the next snap point while snapping is on, else by arrow_step_px (arrow_big_step_px with Shift). `defaults` is what every person starts with; `sections` are the settings rows the Panes settings draw with the shared TuningPanel. A person's own choice is their workspace preference screen-arrangement.pane-sizes and wins key by key.",
